add(0,[
["Case study: matching a week of tasks to tools",[
"Rina is a freelance designer. She lists ten tasks from her week and decides, for each, which tool type fits and how risky an error would be. The judgments are illustrative, but the method is what you will use in your own task map.",
"CODE:TASK | TOOL TYPE | ERROR RISK | PLAN\nDraft a client follow-up email | chat assistant | low | AI drafts, she edits and sends\nFind the current rate for a stock-photo licence | search-enabled assistant | medium | open the vendor page, confirm\nSummarize a 15-page client brief | document tool | medium | AI summary, she checks each requirement against the brief\nBrainstorm 20 project names | chat assistant | low | AI lists, she chooses\nCreate a rough mood-board image | image generator | low | draft only, she creates the final\nTranscribe a client call | transcription tool | medium | check names and numbers\nCalculate project margins | spreadsheet formulas | high | AI explains the formula, she tests it\nDraft a contract clause | not AI | high | lawyer\nTrack hours and invoices | automation platform | medium | rules-based automation, not AI\nReply to an upset client | not AI first | high | she writes it, AI may suggest a calmer rewrite",
"Three lessons stand out. First, only five of the ten tasks use a plain chat assistant, so choosing by task matters. Second, two tasks are better done without AI, and one is better served by ordinary rule-based automation. Third, error risk, not convenience, set how much checking she planned. The tasks she called high-risk got the most human attention."]],
["Case study: one prompt, two assistants",[
"Rina sends the same prompt to two assistants: 'Explain what a token is to a client who is not technical.' The outputs below are written by hand to illustrate how products can differ even on a simple request.",
"CODE:ASSISTANT A (illustrative)\nA token is a small chunk of text the AI reads, often a short word or part of a longer one. Think of it as a Scrabble tile. AI tools count and charge by tokens, which is why very long documents can cost more or get cut off.\n\nASSISTANT B (illustrative)\nTokens are the units into which language models split text for processing. Tokenization depends on the model's vocabulary, and a typical English word averages about 1.3 tokens.",
"CODE:HER COMPARISON NOTES\nA: matched the audience (non-technical), used an analogy, short.\nB: accurate but technical, no analogy, ignored 'client'.\nAccuracy: both fine. Fit to audience: A much better.\nLesson: she should have stated the audience and length. A prompt that says only 'explain tokens' would leave the difference to each product's defaults.",
"Different products wrap models differently, with different hidden instructions and styles. This is why comparing two tools on your own tasks is the only reliable way to choose. It is also why writing a clear prompt matters more than hunting for a better tool."]],
["Guided lab: your glossary, tool map and comparison",[
"Goal: build your vocabulary and a realistic tool map. Allow about 2 hours.",
"CODE:Steps\n1. Write a 20-term glossary in your own words: AI, machine learning, deep learning, generative AI, model, token, context window, prompt, hallucination, bias, multimodal, embedding, agent, RAG, temperature, system prompt, fine-tuning, inference, narrow AI, open model.\n2. List 10 recurring tasks from your week. For each, choose a tool type, rate the error risk (low, medium, high), and write the checking you will do.\n3. Mark any task where AI should not be used first, with a reason.\n4. Judge each myth as true, false or partly true, and explain: AI understands, is always current, is unbiased, a longer prompt is better.\n5. Send one prompt, with the audience stated, to two assistants. Write five sentences on tone, structure and accuracy. Then repeat without the audience and see what changes.\n6. Complete the baseline comparison notes you will revisit in Week 12.",
"CODE:SUCCESS CHECKLIST\n[ ] 20 terms defined without copying a source\n[ ] 10 tasks mapped with risk and a checking plan\n[ ] At least one task marked 'not AI first' with a reason\n[ ] Four myths judged with reasons\n[ ] Two-assistant comparison written, with and without audience\n[ ] Baseline notes saved in your journal\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES\n- Defining terms by copying dictionary wording you could not explain aloud.\n- Rating every task low risk because it is convenient.\n- Comparing two assistants with different prompts, which makes the comparison unfair.",
"A good test of your glossary: explain three terms to a friend without notes. If you stumble, the definition is not yet yours."]]
]);
add(1,[
["Case study: seeing tokens, context and temperature in action",[
"These results are illustrative and written by hand, but the patterns match what learners usually find when they run the experiments. Run your own and compare.",
"CODE:EXPERIMENT 1: TOKEN ESTIMATES\nText | My estimate | Tokenizer | Notes\nShort email (80 words) | 110 | 104 | close\nNews paragraph (120 words) | 160 | 158 | close\nPython snippet (10 lines) | 120 | 175 | code needed more\nSentence in Filipino (15 words) | 25 | 41 | non-English needed more\nTable of numbers | 60 | 112 | numbers split into pieces\n\nLesson: English prose tracks the rule of thumb. Code, other languages and numbers do not. Costs and limits shift accordingly.",
"CODE:EXPERIMENT 2: FINDING A BURIED FACT\nA 6-page document with the line 'The access code is 4471' placed in three different spots across three trials.\nStart of document: found in all runs.\nEnd of document: found in all runs.\nMiddle of document: missed in 2 of 5 runs, or confused with another number.\n\nLesson: very long inputs can blur details in the middle. Ask for quotes, put key facts early or restate them last, and verify.",
"CODE:EXPERIMENT 3: TEMPERATURE\nPrompt: 'Give me a tagline for a bakery.'\nLow randomness, five runs: 'Baked fresh daily' four times, 'Fresh from our oven' once.\nHigher randomness, five runs: five different taglines.\n\nLesson: use low randomness for tasks with one right form, higher for options.\n\nEXPERIMENT 4: STATELESSNESS\nIn chat 1: 'My cat is named Pogo.' In a new chat 2: 'What is my cat's name?'\nAnswer: it does not know. Memory features, where offered, work by re-sending saved notes.",
"Four short experiments explain most of the behavior in this chapter. Notice that none needs any technical skill. They need only a tokenizer page, a long document, and curiosity."]],
["Guided lab: run the four experiments",[
"Goal: see the mechanisms rather than just read about them. Allow about 90 minutes.",
"CODE:Steps\n1. Token estimates: choose five texts (an email, a news paragraph, a code snippet, a non-English sentence, a table). Write your guess for each, then check with a free online tokenizer. Record your error in a table.\n2. Buried fact: take a long document you can edit, or write one. Add a unique fact at the start, middle and end in three separate tests. Ask for the fact each time. Record where recall fails.\n3. Temperature: if you have API access, run one creative prompt five times at low and five times at higher temperature (the practice script 01_token_usage.py does this). Without API access, rerun the same prompt several times in a chat tool and note how much it varies.\n4. Statelessness: tell one chat something, then ask a brand-new chat about it.\n5. Write a paragraph explaining one surprising result, using the vocabulary from this chapter.",
"CODE:SUCCESS CHECKLIST\n[ ] Token table for five texts, with your error\n[ ] Buried-fact test run for start, middle and end\n[ ] Variation observed across repeated runs\n[ ] Statelessness test recorded\n[ ] One paragraph explaining a result with the correct terms\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES\n- Running the context test in a chat full of other messages. Use a fresh one.\n- Treating a single run as proof. Repeat each test several times.\n- Confusing a tool's hidden memory feature with the model remembering.",
"Tool settings differ. If you cannot adjust temperature in your tool, that is information too. Note what the tool offers, and what the practice script lets you do instead."]]
]);
add(11,[
["Sample capstone write-up",[
"This one-page example shows the level of detail to aim for. The task and figures are fictional and written to illustrate a good structure, not to set a target.",
"CODE:CASE STUDY: WEEKLY STATUS REPORT ASSISTANT\n\nPROBLEM\nI spent 45 minutes each week turning scattered notes into a status report. Inconsistent format and the occasional wrong owner were recurring complaints.\n\nBEFORE (4 weeks of timing)\n45 minutes per report. One owner error in 4 reports.\n\nWHAT I BUILT\nA four-step chain: extract (JSON), group by project, draft from the JSON only, critique against the notes. I read the critique and approve before sending. Names are replaced with initials before pasting.\n\nHOW I TESTED IT\n20 test cases written before building: 12 normal, 4 messy, 2 with missing owners, 1 with a hostile instruction, 1 empty. I scored v2, v3 and v4 on accuracy, format and unsupported claims.\n\nAFTER\nv4: accuracy 4.5 of 5, format 4.7, 1 of 20 unsupported claims. Average time 13 minutes including review. Four live weeks: no invented owners.\n\nWHAT DID NOT WORK\nVery short notes still produce vague summaries. The critique step missed one date error that I caught while reading. I still read every report.\n\nNEXT\nAdd a check that flags notes under 30 words, and test on a second team's notes.\n\nPRIVACY\nNo names, clients or figures were pasted. The tool's data settings were reviewed on [date].",
"Notice what makes this convincing. Every claim has a number. The tests were written before the results. The limits and failures are stated plainly. The privacy step is explicit. And it is one page."]],
["Self-grading your capstone",[
"Score your project from 1 to 5 on each row. A score of 4 or above on every row means you have met the course standard. Be honest. The point is to find what to improve.",
"CODE:ROW | WHAT A 5 LOOKS LIKE\nProblem statement | task, user, current cost and numeric success criteria written before building\nProcess map | steps, human checkpoints and risks shown\nPrompts | versioned, with change log and reasons\nStructured output | at least one validated step, with null handling\nEvaluation | 20+ cases, expected answers written first, two versions compared\nSafety and privacy | data handling, hostile-input test and approval points\nResults | before and after numbers, including cost and time\nHonesty | failures and limits stated plainly\nBaseline | day-one tasks redone and compared\nPresentation | one-page case study and a five-minute demo",
"CODE:SCORING\n45 to 50: strong. Share it.\n38 to 44: solid. Fix the lowest two rows.\n30 to 37: incomplete. Add evidence where rows are weakest.\nBelow 30: finish the missing parts before presenting.",
"If you can, ask someone outside the project to read your case study cold. Whatever they find unclear is the next thing to fix."]]
]);
